WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:07.440
- Everyone's got a story to tell. Everyone's got a&nbsp;
great idea. Experiment, you know and if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:15.590
you and a few people are finding this game really&nbsp;fun, 
then I'm sure, like Chris says, that that niche&nbsp;&nbsp;audience will be there.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:25.471
- We founded the company way&nbsp;back in 2008 and how I got into that,
I guess from my graduate course I was doing web design.

00:00:25.471 --> 00:00:36.800
And way back then there was kind of a thing called Flash and I was making
a lot of Flash games for advertising companies for if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.080
someone wanted to create their brand a little game&nbsp;
you could play online, as part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:49.440
the sales pitch. So doing that I was also with&nbsp;
my business partner Luke, he was also in a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:57.040
career, making these flash&nbsp;
games and we just, you know, we enjoyed

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.960
doing this but we wanted to strike&nbsp;
out on our own. So we carried on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.760
making games for clients but in the&nbsp;
background we were kind of making our own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.840
game. And even at that time, we were making it&nbsp;
not sure what we were going to do with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.680
But we just felt like it would be really like,
we wanted to make games slightly differently. A lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:24.960
of our games have hand crafted elements. And then&nbsp;
so while making these client based games we made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:32.480
a game called Lume, which was all made by paper&nbsp;
and craft with an animated character running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:39.440
around. We filmed it in a friend's bedroom really&nbsp;
really low budget, really low scale and when we did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:46.320
that we then decided to let's just put it on Steam&nbsp;
and see what happens. Back then Steam you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.480
only had a handful of indie games and we were&nbsp;
lucky enough around that like 2010, 2011 kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:02:01.440
first independent game companies to be in that&nbsp;
bunch. And so Lume went on to kind of pay for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.640
itself and more and gave us this independence that&nbsp;
we didn't know we could do. We could make a game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:14.480
in this small studio and we can we can launch&nbsp;it.
- I started in the industry back in '96.&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:23.120
I'd always wanted to work in games so I managed to&nbsp;
land a job in a what we would now call it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:29.680
little indie company straight out of university&nbsp;
and that gave me a leg up to get a job at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.280
EA for a couple of years. I worked at&nbsp;
Intelligent Games and then around about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.960
the millennium I settled down at Traveller's Tales&nbsp;
just before they released the first LEGO Star Wars.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:50.800
So over the next 15 years or so, I worked on most&nbsp;
of the LEGO series up until the point where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:56.480
finally did LEGO Dimensions. I spent a lot&nbsp;
of time working on the Doctor Who elements of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:04.400
LEGO Dimensions. And then I felt I'd peaked
so set up uh my company, Quantum Soup Studios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:14.640
in 2016 and t's been an interesting
experience, a little bit rocky. My original

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.920
game I couldn't manage to find a publisher for&nbsp;
so we had to pivot to do a bit of work for hire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.680
And then our current project which
I'm gonna have to be cagey about because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:31.200
unannounced, our current project is a licensed&nbsp;
game that I did manage to get a publisher for.&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:38.640
So it's been a little bit of a windy road
to where we are today but we're currently working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.720
on a licensed game with a big IP for families&nbsp;
which I'm very excited about.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:52.720
- What do you think were the benefits of striking out and
starting&nbsp;on your own? What was the difference between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.360
working for your own studio and working&nbsp;
for another agency, like TT or EA?

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:08.400
- Yeah it's swings and roundabouts. There are pros and&nbsp;
cons. My lead at TT had run a company for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:16.560
a while and unfortunately it had, through&nbsp;
no fault of his, it had failed.&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:26.400
I'd never experienced that so I'd only had that laser&nbsp;
focus and I was keen to have a chance to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:32.560
more creative decisions. So I was quite excited&nbsp;
to be able to go like right this is the

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.800
thing that I want to do. That also was&nbsp;
a bit of a learning experience because the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:46.240
thing that I wanted to do was a bit quirky&nbsp;
and a bit too ambitious and as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:54.400
I couldn't land a&nbsp;deal for that one.
So I had to um pivot and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:00.000
do things differently for a little bit.
And that was a little bit of a difficult

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:09.440
time because you know, it made me question some&nbsp;
of my decisions. But I feel like I was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:16.320
turn it around, so this this project feels like&nbsp;
it's a much better bet commercially

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.240
than that original one. I want to go back to that&nbsp;
original one because I still think that

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.320
it's got legs but it's something that I think that&nbsp;
I'm probably going to have to put some of my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.655
money into rather than trying to pitch it to&nbsp;
somebody else.

00:05:28.655 --> 00:05:37.040
- The benefits were again, and Chris has sort of touched on this,
the creative freedom&nbsp;you have with creating your own

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:43.520
project. And, you know, whatever happens it's&nbsp;
on you. I mean it's tough and I always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:49.680
think the beginning of the game process is very&nbsp;
exciting and you think-- But getting a game over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:57.360
line and published and you know you've got
scheduling issues, HR issues, you've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:04.080
is this even a good idea anymore issues, you&nbsp;
know, that's really tough. And it's&nbsp;very...

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.760
it can be very stressful and you always need&nbsp;
to kind of make sure you're looking after

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:18.560
yourself and your team. And you know, 
yeah that's really tough. We self-publish as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:25.200
well so we're not even relying on a publisher
for our games. I mean that that kind of freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:32.080
and I think with the knowledge and the, you know,
doing it 15 years we kind of... we hope we know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:39.760
to make a State of Play game and how
to get it done. So it's really exciting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:47.920
kind of hey I want to tell a story and I want&nbsp;
an audience to see it. So yeah that was a&nbsp;real benefit for us.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.480
- I'm going to start with Chris&nbsp;on this
and if it's not too brutal a question

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:58.400
you know, what does it cost to set up on your own?
Do you need a savings pot? Do you think you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.800
to have a day job? I'm sure you don't because you&nbsp;didn't.
- I think one of the mistakes that I made was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:08.240
I was a little bit too ambitious early on. I was&nbsp;
targeting sort of like projects that would

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:17.280
have a budget of about half a mil and that sits&nbsp;
in a weird no man's land between micro indie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:23.520
games where publishers are quite happy to throw&nbsp;
sort of like 50 grand or 100 grand at, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:30.080
a solo developer who's got a really cool quirky&nbsp;
project, because that's pocket change to them.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:35.760
But once you get up to the like half a mil&nbsp;
to a mill that gets into the range where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:40.640
it's still a cheap project in the&nbsp;
scheme of things, it sounds like a lot,

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:48.000
it goes in no time. They're not really&nbsp;
interested until you get up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.920
the sort of like six to ten million budget&nbsp;
where you then need sort of like a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:58.080
team and that's complicated. I tried to sort&nbsp;
of go into that middle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:04.240
that no man's land because I didn't really have
a lot of experience with project budgets.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:10.960
That felt like it would be a comfortable&nbsp;
sort of like team size and budget size

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:18.560
for me at the time. But it didn't really fit with&nbsp;
what publishers were looking for and also it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:25.200
carried a bit of a risk because I was trying to&nbsp;
go from full-time employment to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:30.720
full-time employment and I needed to have some&nbsp;
sort of budget in place. I think it's probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.720
smarter to do it the way that Katherine has done&nbsp;
it, which is like starting with something that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:44.160
a working business first and then&nbsp;
transitioning into into something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:51.840
more creative gradually without taking such a big&nbsp;risk.
I don't know if you agree with that Kath.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:57.440
- I don't know. I don't know if we've done it the right&nbsp;
way. You'll have to tell us but but yeah I mean again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:03.040
like I mentioned before, I guess generally&nbsp;
as a company we've been quite risk averse and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:11.200
quite early on I've got an excellent mentor&nbsp;
in the industry who's a veteran games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:17.200
developer and he just turned to me and went keep&nbsp;
your overheads low. And it's probably the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:23.920
piece of advice someone's ever given me because&nbsp;
basically yeah each project we you know.... with teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:29.520
of two, if that goes well with team of four. 
So we've scaled up in a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:36.720
manageable way and we haven't relied&nbsp;
on publishing money. It's been our own money but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:44.560
then again the profits have all been our own money.
So yeah we've really scaled up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:52.320
that way and it it seems to work&nbsp;for us.
- It actually leads us on quite nicely to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.840
next section which is about investors. I think&nbsp;
that's an area that lots of budding developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:03.760
feel that there's quite a lot of information&nbsp;
about investing out there. So if you google how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:10.000
to find investment you'll be in inundated with&nbsp;
with helpful articles. But it's also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:16.320
bit of a mystery because we all know&nbsp;
that these things are quite often fortuitous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:20.080
it's quite often to do with connections,  it's quite&nbsp;
often to do with someone you may have bumped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.600
in with, to someone at GDC or something. Did you&nbsp;
set up a vertical slice of your game and then&nbsp;take that round?

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:32.560
We've worked a lot&nbsp;with with platforms such as Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:37.920
and again, when we're sort of showing&nbsp;
them the next game, we've done things like vertical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:44.320
slice of the game and a pitch. And so that's&nbsp;
probably how we get them interested in the game.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:52.320
Showing as much as you can is always great but&nbsp;
again, don't invest a year of your time into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:57.200
game that you're then gonna pitch. 
It's sort of getting the balance right

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:03.600
about giving enough to get people interested but&nbsp;
obviously you don't want to be working for free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:09.840
for years on a game hoping to get some investment.
- Once I started pitching games, I did find that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:18.080
the fact that I got like 20 years of experience in&nbsp;
the industry didn't really count for very much

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:25.280
in the context of pitching a particular game. 
It&nbsp;was all about okay so what's the game,

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:33.680
who's it for, have you got a demo that we can&nbsp;
play. That is all they're interested in.&nbsp;

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:40.560
I suspect that sort of like my background did help&nbsp;
kind of gloss over a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:47.040
questions that they might have asked a&nbsp;
less experienced developer but it certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:51.760
didn't just magically open the&nbsp;
door sort of like oh okay you know what you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:58.320
doing then, great have some money which is kind of&nbsp;
what I was hoping for. And that very much did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:06.240
not happen. So you have to show what you&nbsp;
want to make. Vertical slices

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:13.360
and just demos in general are a bit&nbsp;
of a pain but it is so useful to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:18.960
build something like that so that you can
show it to people and get feedback on it.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:26.000
It is very satisfying to have people&nbsp;
play your demo and go like yes,

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:32.480
I would definitely buy that game, when's it done?
It's like when somebody will pay us to finish it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:37.840
- The next question I'd like to ask you&nbsp;
about is marketing and finding an audience.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:44.640
Gaming is an exceptionally competitive and&nbsp;
in some instances, and I say this with love, over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:52.320
saturated markets particularly for certain games&nbsp;
in certain sub-genres. So I'd love to know how...

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:57.078
Do you start with an audience, an ideal audience and
work your way&nbsp;backwards? 

00:12:57.078 --> 00:13:02.043
Do you start with the game idea and&nbsp; then try and hope
you find an audience? How do&nbsp;you go about that?

00:13:02.043 --> 00:13:06.480
- I guess for us, we kind of start&nbsp;
with the idea first. I mean, I'd like to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:12.000
we're never just kind of making it in a vacuum&nbsp;
of like this is a game we want to make you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.600
don't care if anyone likes it. Obviously you're&nbsp;
always trying to make a game that there is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:22.400
audience for. But I think a really good tip
is to be authentic to yourself and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:29.440
if you are just kind of playing a numbers game of&nbsp;
like well 18 to 24 year olds like these so we need--&nbsp;

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:34.160
you know, it's gonna come out in the end result&nbsp;
and you're not gonna enjoy making it either.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:39.280
A game takes a long time to make and you&nbsp;
know, make sure it's something you want to wake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:47.440
up every morning to and make it.
The biggest marketing tool for us if I'm being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:54.320
really honest is awards and especially the&nbsp;
BAFTA we won from Lumino City. We got that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.320 --> 00:14:04.160
before it launched on IOS. So it was on PC,
won a BAFTA and two months later came out on&nbsp;IOS.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:11.200
So straight away it was a BAFTA award-winning&nbsp;
game you know. IT kind of told its own story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:18.000
which we were incredibly grateful&nbsp;for.
So for people starting out, I really recommend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:22.460
if you feel like your game's good, it's&nbsp;
always worth a shot at just entering.&nbsp;

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:31.280
I'm interested in making games. I have no particular&nbsp;
sort of skill at selling them.

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:38.240
So my plan has always been to work&nbsp;
with a publisher who knows what they're doing.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:44.160
The publishers have the budget&nbsp;
and the knowledge to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:51.840
a given project in front of the audience who&nbsp;
will be interested in it. What Katherine says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.840 --> 00:15:00.080
is right though about you generally don't&nbsp;
want to go chasing a trend or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:04.640
because that is going to put you in competition&nbsp;
with literally everybody else. If your ambition

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:10.640
is to try to compete&nbsp;with Fortnite
that's a high bar to hit.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:16.400
So as a small company, particularly as a start-up&nbsp;
company, you're better off doing something that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:22.320
a bit different. You can find a niche&nbsp;
audience but a niche audience is usually enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:29.520
support a small company. And you can grow from&nbsp;
there. But actually doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:34.960
something a little bit different is a good&nbsp;way to be known.
- There's no point trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:41.120
copy an amazing game idea that's already&nbsp;
out there doing really well. Well there's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:46.640
point copying any game because it's already out there.
Everyone's got a story to tell.

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:54.160
Everyone's got a great idea. Experiment,
you know. And if you and a few people are finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.160 --> 00:16:07.840
this game really fun, then I'm sure, like Chris&nbsp;
says, that that niche audience will be there.

